<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<subsection xml:id="subsec-num-project-methods">
  <title>Statistical Methods and Computations</title>

  <p>
    Complete three analyses: one analysis of a single population mean,
    one hypothesis test comparing two means, and a one-way ANOVA. Use R
    to perform the analyses and present the information needed to
    understand and evaluate your work.
  </p>

  <paragraphs xml:id="num-project-method-one-mean">
    <title>Analysis 1: One Population Mean</title>
    <p>
      Choose either a one-mean <m>t</m> confidence interval
      or a one-mean <m>t</m> hypothesis test. Either option
      satisfies this component. You may include both when they address
      your research question.
    </p>
    <p>
      For the confidence-interval option, state the confidence level and
      report the interval endpoints with units. For the hypothesis-test
      option, justify the benchmark <m>\mu_0</m>, state <m>H_0</m> and
      <m>H_a</m>, state the significance level, and report the <m>t</m>
      statistic, degrees of freedom, and <m>p</m>-value. For a one-sample
      <m>t</m> procedure, the degrees of freedom are <m>n-1</m>.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="num-project-method-two-means">
    <title>Analysis 2: A Hypothesis Test Comparing Two Means</title>
    <p>
      Choose either a paired <m>t</m> hypothesis test or
      an independent-samples Welch <m>t</m> hypothesis test.
      The structure of the data determines the appropriate procedure.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Paired option:</alert> Explain the pairing and define the
      difference, such as <m>d=\text{after}-\text{before}</m>. Define
      <m>\mu_d</m> as the population mean difference in that order. State hypotheses about <m>\mu_d</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Independent option:</alert> Identify group 1 and group 2,
      define <m>\mu_1-\mu_2</m> in that order, and explain why the
      samples are independent. State hypotheses about the difference in
      population means and assess the distribution and sample size
      within each group. Report <m>t</m>,
      the Welch degrees of freedom from R, and the <m>p</m>-value.
    </p>
    <p>
      For either option, state the significance level and use a test
      direction justified by your research question. Keep the subtraction
      order consistent in the hypotheses, R analysis, and interpretation.
      The hypothesis test fulfills this component; a two-mean confidence
      interval is optional.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="num-project-method-anova">
    <title>Analysis 3: One-Way ANOVA</title>
    <p>
      Compare the population mean of a numerical response across
      three or more independent groups. Identify the
      response, units, grouping variable, and group labels. In R, make
      sure that group labels are treated as a factor, including when
      they are stored as numerical codes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Define <m>\mu_i</m> as the population mean for group <m>i</m>,
      where <m>k</m> is the number of groups. State the hypotheses.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use a classical one-way ANOVA to obtain the
      ANOVA table. Include the relevant table and
      report the significance level, <m>F</m> statistic, numerator and
      denominator degrees of freedom, and <m>p</m>-value. For <m>N</m>
      total observations across <m>k</m> groups, the degrees of freedom
      are <m>k-1</m> and <m>N-k</m>, respectively. Use the observations
      actually included in the fitted model when identifying <m>N</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the ANOVA is statistically significant, use
      <alert>Tukey comparisons</alert> on the
      ANOVA model. Report the adjusted <m>p</m>-values, identify
      significant pairs, and describe the direction and size of the
      observed mean differences. Compare adjusted <m>p</m>-values
      directly with your significance level. If the ANOVA is not
      statistically significant, state that result and explain why
      follow-up comparisons are not pursued for this project.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>
</subsection>
